                         CONFERENCE ADVISORY

    DIGITAL PREVIEWS FIRST INDUSTRIAL ALPHA-BASED COMPUTER SYSTEM

Digital Equipment Corporation will unveil a new 64-bit Alpha-based 
system designed specifically for industrial use at the Telecom & 
Industrial PCI Conference in Framingham, Massachusetts on December 
3, 1996.

This announcement of another offering in Digital's family of  Alpha 
and Intel Pentium computer systems strengthens Digital's performance 
leadership in the industrial and manufacturing markets.

The industrial Alpha computer is based on a 266 MHz Alpha Single 
Board Computer (SBC) packaged in a protective enclosure which 
conforms to NEMA 12 specifications, allowing users to access 
superior computing power where they need it, and guaranteeing 
reliable operation even in harsh industrial environments. The SBC is 
part of Digital's Modular Computing Components family.

64-bit Alpha versions of the industrial computers will support 
processor speeds in excess of 300 MHz, and will run on Windows NT,  
Digital UNIX, OpenVMS, and VxWorks, providing users on the factory 
floor with faster computer response times for data-intensive 
applications. With Intel Pentium offerings of speeds ranging up to 
200 MHz, these versions will support MS-DOS and Windows, Windows NT, 
and VxWorks. The use of the peripheral component interconnect (PCI) 
Industrial Computer Manufacturer's Group (PICMG)  CPU card standard 
makes it easy to upgrade to higher speed CPUs, CPUs from other 
vendors, or CPUs with a different processor architecture. The same 
enclosure can be retained as new technology becomes available in the 
future.

The industrial computer system is designed to meet the demands of 
the harshest industrial environments. The rackmountable enclosure is 
constructed of cold-rolled steel with a shock and vibration isolated 
drive cage, and is available with a NEMA 12 front panel to withstand 
humidity, particulate-laden air, water, and other harsh elements. A 
NEMA 4 model provides an added degree of protection against dust, 
splashing liquids, and hose-directed water.

These industrial systems from Digital are available for ordering now 
with first shipments expected by April 1997. Prices are 
custom-quoted.
