                        Digital Supports New
                  Information Technology Agreement


   ...Agreement will Spur Demand for Technology Products Worldwide


MAYNARD, Mass., Dec. 13, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
praised President Clinton and United States Trade Representative 
Charlene Barshefsky for their leadership in reaching agreement at 
the World Trade Organization meeting in Singapore to eliminate 
tariffs on information technology products.        

     Digital Chairman Robert Palmer said, "The Information 
Technology Agreement will spur demand for Digital's products and 
other information technology products worldwide.  Digital's trade 
priority always has been to eliminate tariffs, because they do not 
make sense in a globalized marketplace.  The ITA will go most of the 
way to achieve that goal."

     In addition to lowering the cost of delivering IT products to 
customers, the ITA will alleviate threats to raise barriers against 
information technology trade through the wrongful reclassification 
of computer products as high-tariff consumer electronics and 
telecommunications products.  The ITA should avoid major trade 
conflicts over computer classification.

     Palmer concluded, "Although important details remain to be 
finalized, the outcome in Singapore gives us tremendous optimism 
that tariffs will soon be a relic of the past.  Digital commends 
President Clinton and USTR Barshefsky for bringing us to this 
point." 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
